    <div class="article-copy">
      <p>
        When a show feels effortless, there is usually a detailed plan behind
        it. Technical production turns an idea into a sequence of practical
        decisions, shared responsibilities and carefully prepared moments.
      </p>
      <h2>Bring the teams together early.</h2>
      <p>
        Lighting, sound, video, staging and rigging affect one another.
        Reviewing them together helps establish what the space can support and
        where the creative ambition needs a more detailed solution.
      </p>
      <p>
        A useful starting point is a shared view of the event format, audience,
        venue, content requirements and available rehearsal time.
      </p>
      <h2>Turn the plan into a running order.</h2>
      <p>
        A running order connects the audience experience to the work happening
        behind the scenes. It should make it clear what happens, who is
        responsible and what each department needs to prepare.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Map arrivals, presentations, transitions and breaks.</li>
        <li>Confirm content formats and delivery deadlines.</li>
        <li>Allow time to test key moments with the people delivering them.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="editorial-pullquote">
        <span class="cue" style="--gel: #11bfdd">A practical principle</span
        >Clarity behind the scenes gives the live experience room to breathe.
      </div>
      <h2>Rehearse the transitions.</h2>
      <p>
        The spaces between headline moments deserve attention too. Speaker
        changes, walk-on music, video playback and lighting cues all shape the
        pace of a show.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rehearsal creates a chance to check those connections, agree
        communication and review alternative approaches before the audience
        arrives.
      </p>
